  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nA stronger front looks to arrive sometime Saturday night and Sunday \nwith rain chances on the increase as we go into Saturday afternoon \nand evening with the approach of a mid to upper-level trough that \nwill become positively tilted as it digs into parts of northern \nMexico. The enhanced lift with the trough along with the embedded \nshortwaves associated with it will provide chances for multiple \nrounds of showers and thunderstorms as we go into the overnight \nhours of Saturday and into all day Sunday. The latest HRRR and REFS \nensemble runs show PWAT values between the 1.8-2.0 inch range across \nour area which would support locally heavy rainfall. 12Z model \nsoundings show a fairly saturated thermodynamics profile throughout \nthe boundary layer with a relatively skinny CAPE profile as well \nwhich is conducive for a classic heavy rainfall setup. As a result, \nthe WPC has all of our CWA in a level 2(slight risk) for excessive \nrainfall beginning Sunday morning and into Monday. Rainfall totals \nfrom Saturday through Monday will range from around 1-3 inches \nacross the Coastal Bend with isolated amounts closer to 4 inches. \nHigher rainfall totals will exist farther west across the Brush \nCountry and Rio Grande Plains with totals between 2-4 inches with\nisolated amounts of around 5 inches in and around where any \ntraining of storms setup. A likely hindrance to a higher end \nrainfall event for Sunday is due to the northerly flow from the \nrecent frontal passage which would aid in more stable conditions \nin the form of moderate to periods of heavy rainfall with a few \nembedded thunderstorms via Isentropic Lift. Instability will be \nweak in combination with preexisting cloudiness and precipitation,\nwhich would further hinder a higher end rainfall event. Rain \nchances will begin to taper off as we go into Monday afternoon and\nevening as the trough progresses northeast of our area and \neventually de-amplifies across the Eastern US towards Midweek. A \nbrief quiet period will exist on Tuesday before rain chances \nreturn once more on Wednesday as the subtropical jet redevelops \nacross the state, resulting in low rain chances(10-30%) across our\narea. A cold front looks to push through sometime on Thursday \nbringing slightly cooler temperatures with a decent drop in \nhumidity levels.\n\nFor our coastlines, we are monitoring the potential for minor \ncoastal flooding as we go into this weekend and early next week. \nTides will also be elevated in conjunction with the coastal \nflooding with the arrival of a new moon phase. High Tides cycles \nfor Saturday are forecast to reach 2.0 ft above MSL with Sunday \nand Monday's high tide levels near 2.5 ft above MSL due to a \nmoderate to strong persistent onshore flow.",
